## Findings

#### 1. Enum.ToString() + ToLowerInvariant() per time part — compound allocation (called per unit per Humanize call)
**Impact:** 3 allocations per time unit: `Enum.ToString()` (reflection + string), `.ToLowerInvariant()` (new string), then `$"{value} {unitName}"` (interpolated string). At millions of calls/min, this is the #1 allocator.
**Files:** `TimeSpanFormatting.cs:L175-176`
**Fix:** Cache unit names in a static array indexed by `(int)TimeUnit`:
```csharp
private static readonly string[] s_unitNames =
    ["year", "month", "week", "day", "hour", "minute", "second", "millisecond"];

// In FormatTimePart:
var unitName = s_unitNames[(int)unit];
return value == 1 ? $"{value} {unitName}" : $"{value} {unitName}s";
```
Could go further and cache the plural forms too (`"years"`, `"months"`, …) to eliminate even the interpolated string for the suffix.

#### 2. LINQ chains in SetPrecisionOfTimeSpan — up to 3 enumerator + 2 delegate allocations per call (🔴 Critical on hot path)
**Impact:** `.Where().Take().Where()` creates 3 lazy enumerators and 2 delegate allocations, then `[.. timeParts]` materializes into a new `List<string?>`. All per call.
**Files:** `TimeSpanFormatting.cs:L133-149`
**Fix:** Replace with a single `foreach` loop:
```csharp
private static List<string?> SetPrecisionOfTimeSpan(
    List<string?> timeParts, int precision, bool countEmptyUnits)
{
    var result = new List<string?>(precision);
    int count = 0;
    foreach (var part in timeParts)
    {
        if (count >= precision) break;
        if (!countEmptyUnits && part == null) continue;
        if (countEmptyUnits) count++;
        if (part != null)
        {
            if (!countEmptyUnits) count++;
            result.Add(part);
        }
    }
    return result;
}
```

#### 3. LINQ .Any() with delegate for null check (🔴 Critical on hot path)
**Impact:** Allocates an enumerator and a delegate on every call. Easily replaced with a loop.
**Files:** `TimeSpanFormatting.cs:L127-128`
**Fix:** Replace with a foreach loop:
```csharp
private static bool IsContainingOnlyNullValue(List<string?> timeParts)
{
    foreach (var part in timeParts)
        if (part != null) return false;
    return true;
}
```
**Caveat:** Also change parameter type from `IEnumerable<string?>` to `List<string?>` to avoid boxing the struct enumerator.

#### 4. .Select().Where().ToArray() in HumanizeDisplayStrings — 3 allocations per call (🟡 Moderate)
**Impact:** Enumerator + 2 delegates + intermediate array. Called on every `DefaultCollectionFormatter.Humanize` invocation.
**Files:** `TimeSpanFormatting.cs:L269-272`
**Fix:** Replace with a foreach loop building a `List<string>` (or use `stackalloc` + `ArrayPool` for the index list):
```csharp
private string HumanizeDisplayStrings(IEnumerable<string?> strings, string separator)
{
    var items = new List<string>();
    foreach (var item in strings)
    {
        if (item == null) continue;
        var trimmed = item.Trim();
        if (!string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(trimmed))
            items.Add(trimmed);
    }
    // ... rest of formatting
}
```

